          { name: "Youth Engagement and Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.2 median age and 34.5% renter rate together signal a district with meaningful youth representation facing real constraints on housing, educational attainment, and economic mobility. Young adults in this district are evaluating whether government and local leadership are creating pathways to better economic circumstances or perpetuating the same constraints their parents faced. A candidate who addresses youth unemployment, entry-level wages, housing affordability, and post-secondary educational pathways is engaging the demographic constituency most likely to swing based on material improvements rather than inherited partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Black Community Engagement and Representation", tag: "careful", why: "At 57% of the district, Black voters are the plurality constituency, yet the 66% Democratic margin in a majority-Black district suggests either incomplete Black voter mobilization or meaningful Black crossover support to Republicans. Any candidate seeking to hold or win this seat must understand specifically which of these dynamics is occurring and what it reflects about Black constituent satisfaction with current representation. The 37.2% college attainment and $64,828 income indicate a Black constituency with economic credentials and constraints that deserve specific policy engagement, not tokenism or assumption that Black voters will automatically support a particular candidate or party." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result in a 57% Black-plurality district is not overwhelming — it reflects the Democratic candidate's ability to consolidate Black voters, non-Black minority voters, and persuadable non-Black residents. In a district where white residents are 36% of the population and Black residents 57%, any erosion in Black voter participation or consolidation, or any increase in Republican performance among white or other non-Black voters, is sufficient to narrow the margin significantly. Treating 66% as a secure incumbent position rather than as a coalition that requires active constituent engagement invites a credible challenger to build an alternative message that fractures the Democratic base." },
          { name: "Assuming Non-Black Constituencies Are Secondary or Negligible", tag: "avoid", why: "White residents at 36% and other non-Black residents (Asian 1.89%, Hispanic 1.68%, and the ~2.3% gap likely AIAN) together comprise 43% of the district. In a 66-34 Democratic district, the Republican 34% is almost certainly anchored in non-Black constituencies, and these voters are not marginal to the district's electoral outcome — they are the swing population whose satisfaction or dissatisfaction determines whether a 66% margin holds or erodes. Any candidate or incumbent who treats white, Asian, Hispanic, or other non-Black constituencies as secondary to the Black plurality is ceding persuadable voters to the opposition and misreading the district's actual competitive geography." }
