        issues: [
          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 37.2% of the district renting at a median household income of $51,056, housing cost burden is the primary financial squeeze on working-class households. The combination of high renter concentration and constrained income creates a constituency for which housing policy is not abstract — it is whether a family can afford to stay in their neighborhood and maintain stability. Legislative action on rent increases, tenant protections, and affordable housing development is directly legible to constituent experience and is the type of concrete delivery that either solidifies a 59% base or allows it to erode when opposition candidates demonstrate attention to the same issue." },
          { name: "Education Funding and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 17.4% college attainment and 36.7-year median age — indicating school-age children and young adults — the district is a community evaluating whether K-12 investment and job training actually improve economic mobility. Detroit's school funding and workforce development policy are not peripheral to this district's political economy; they are central to whether constituents believe their children have genuine pathways to family-sustaining employment. A representative who can demonstrate measurable gains in graduation rates, vocational training access, and entry-level wage outcomes builds the constituent relationship that holds a 59% base." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost Burden", tag: "careful", why: "Healthcare cost is a driver of financial instability for working-class households, particularly in a district with 37.2% renters who often lack employer-sponsored coverage. Medicaid policy, prescription drug affordability, and primary care access in under-resourced urban neighborhoods are issues where constituent experience is shaped by state legislative action. A representative who takes healthcare cost seriously can build credibility with the working-class base; one who treats it as secondary to other priorities will find constituents testing whether opposition candidates offer better solutions." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Without Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "At 59% in a majority-Black, majority-working-class urban district, this margin reflects a constituency evaluating based on material governance outcomes, not inherited partisan identity. A representative who campaigns on ideological abstractions while housing costs rise, school funding remains constrained, and healthcare access remains difficult for low-income households risks erosion toward opposition candidates who claim to offer concrete solutions. The gap between 59% and the 70%+ margins typical of urban Black Democratic constituencies is persuadable voters asking whether this representative is actually delivering." },
          { name: "Neglecting the 41% Opposition and Its Composition", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this close in a major urban area, understanding what drives the 41% opposition is strategically urgent. Whether opposition comes from working-class voters frustrated by education or housing outcomes, from higher-income voters, or from lower-propensity voters who have disengaged from the process, each requires a different response. A representative who assumes the 59% is stable without understanding specifically what the 41% represents — and what concerns might move them — is gambling with a margin that is not arithmetically as safe as it appears in a deep-blue state." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Detroit's Majority-Black Urban Core at 59% Democratic: Low College Attainment and Income Constraints Require Concrete Constituent Delivery, Not Ideological Positioning",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MI-HD-14 is a majority-Black urban district (45.39%) with a 59-41 Democratic margin that reflects a solid but not overwhelming base in a state and region where Democratic margins in urban cores often run significantly higher. The strategic puzzle is not why the district is Democratic — it is why the margin is only 59% in a constituency where Black voters represent nearly half the population and Democrats hold the state house. With a median household income of $51,056, median age of 36.7, and college attainment of only 17.4%, this is a working-class, younger urban community facing material economic constraints. The 37.2% renter rate signals housing instability and cost burden concerns that are not abstract policy issues but immediate household realities. A 59% margin in this demographic context is not a secure baseline; it reflects a constituency that is evaluating based on whether government is delivering tangible improvement to their economic lives, not inherited partisan loyalty.",
          "The path to holding or expanding this seat runs through specific constituent delivery on the issues that shape daily life for working-class Detroit households: housing affordability and stability, wage and employment quality, healthcare access and cost, and public education funding. With 17.4% college attainment — the lowest education level in the income-constrained category — workforce development and the pathway from high school to family-sustaining employment are direct governance concerns. The 36.7-year median age indicates a constituency with school-age children and young adults at the stages of life when housing costs, childcare affordability, and education investment become acute. The 37.2% renter rate means more than one-third of the district is exposed to housing market volatility and has no asset hedge against income disruption. A representative who can point to concrete legislative wins on these issues — housing policy that stabilizes rents, education funding that improves outcomes in under-resourced schools, healthcare access that reduces cost burden — is defending a 59% base that might otherwise drift. A representative who speaks in ideological abstractions while these material conditions persist will find that 59% erodes as constituents test whether an alternative can actually deliver."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with housing affordability and renter stability: at 37.2% renter rate in a district with $51,056 median income, housing cost burden is the primary financial stress point for a substantial plurality of constituents; legislative wins on rent stabilization, tenant protections, and affordable housing development are directly legible to the district's material concerns.",
          "Build a specific education and workforce development agenda: with 17.4% college attainment and a 36.7-year median age, the district is home to school-age children and young adults evaluating whether K-12 education and job training actually improve their economic trajectory; demonstrate measurable improvement in graduation rates, apprenticeship access, and wage outcomes.",
          "Address healthcare cost and access directly: working-class households at $51,056 income experience healthcare cost burden as a driver of financial instability; Medicaid policy, prescription drug affordability, and primary care access in under-resourced neighborhoods are governance issues that have immediate household impact.",
          "Recognize that 59% is not a ceiling: in a majority-Black urban district with this income and education profile, Democratic margins often run 70%+ when constituents believe government is delivering; the gap between 59% and 70%+ is persuadable voters testing whether this representative is actually improving their material conditions or simply holding partisan position."
        ]
