          { name: "Economic Opportunity for Renters & Working Families", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.5% renter rate and $67,339 median income together signal a working-class constituency that is economically vulnerable to housing cost inflation and job precarity. At 38-year median age, this district includes both young families building assets and older workers protecting fixed incomes — both constituencies facing real pressure in a high-cost environment. A legislative agenda focused on affordable housing, living wage employment, childcare access, and credential pathways into stable work directly addresses the material concerns of a substantial portion of HD-16's voters and offers concrete differentiation from opponents who treat these issues abstractly." },
          { name: "White Community Engagement & Representation", tag: "careful", why: "The 34.43% white population represents roughly one-third of the district, yet the 77% Democratic margin in a district at 57% Black suggests the white community is substantially split or disengaged from the Democratic incumbent. With 23% Republican support district-wide, the Republican base is likely concentrated among white voters — meaning any Democratic incumbent who holds this seat must actively engage and retain white voter support, particularly older homeowners and working-class families whose economic concerns may not align with party messaging that prioritizes racial justice. Treating the white community as a secondary or hostile constituency is arithmetically dangerous in a 57-43 minority-majority district." },
          { name: "Messaging as Identity Politics Rather Than Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% margin in a 57% Black district is substantial but not overwhelming — and it reflects a coalition that includes Black voters evaluating on economic delivery, not exclusively racial solidarity. Any representative who treats the seat as secure based on demographic majority rather than on tangible policy results for working families, renters, and wage-earners will find that margin vulnerable to a challenger who credibly addresses the material concerns of the district's working-class constituency across racial lines. The 23% Republican opposition is not insignificant and represents a reservoir of voters whose defection or mobilization could narrow margins in a future cycle." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 25.5% Renter Community & Treating Housing as a Fringe Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "One in four HD-16 households are renters — approximately 22,000+ households — yet housing affordability and renter protections are often marginalized in legislative agendas that prioritize homeowner issues. For a constituency where $67,339 median income must cover rising housing costs, a representative who does not lead with concrete solutions on rent stabilization, habitability standards, and down-payment assistance programs is leaving a significant constituency's material concerns unaddressed. In a district where the Democratic margin is 74%, allowing housing insecurity among renters to become a mobilizing issue for opposition candidates is a strategic failure." }
