          { name: "Education Funding and School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 54.7% college attainment — the second-highest education level in the district cohort — and a median age of 47 skewed toward established families, education quality is the primary lens through which this constituency evaluates government competence. School funding adequacy, teacher compensation, and measurable academic outcomes are not abstract policy positions but direct household concerns for a demographic that made education investment a core life decision. Any candidate, regardless of party, who cannot speak with specificity to school funding mechanisms, property tax dynamics, and their impact on district schools will lose credibility with this constituency." },
          { name: "Fiscal Discipline and Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $109,090 median household income with 80.9% homeownership, this constituency has significant fixed costs in property and carries real sensitivity to tax burden and government spending efficiency. Property tax fairness, municipal budget transparency, and demonstration that government spending delivers measurable returns are the fiscal concerns that drive this district's political behavior. The 52% Democratic margin suggests Koleszar has consolidated fiscal credibility with this base, but any challenger who can credibly argue superior fiscal management or property tax protection has a genuine opening in a district where affluent homeowners are the plurality constituency." },
          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Political Volatility", tag: "careful", why: "The 52-48 Democratic margin in a district with 54.7% college education and $109K income reflects the well-documented 2020s realignment of college-educated suburban voters away from Republicans — but at 52%, this is a fragile consolidation, not a durable realignment. College-educated suburban voters have shown themselves willing to ticket-split and support fiscally conservative candidates when they perceive government failure on their primary concerns (schools, taxes, quality of life), and this district's demographics make it analytically sensitive to candidate-specific performance rather than partisan default. A credible Republican who leads on fiscal management and school quality — without cultural wedge positioning — could close this four-point gap." },
          { name: "Treating Affluent, Educated Voters as a Reliable Democratic Bloc", tag: "avoid", why: "The historical default of affluent, college-educated suburban voters toward Republican candidates held for decades, and the current 52-48 Democratic advantage is recent and dependent on specific candidate and messaging success. Democratic overconfidence that college education and income have created a permanent constituency shift is the most common error in districts like MI-HD-22, where the actual voter behavior reveals volatility rather than lock-in. Any Democratic incumbent or candidate who campaigns as though 52% represents a stable base will be surprised by Republican gains in education-quality and fiscal-discipline messaging." },
          { name: "Cultural or Identity-Based Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "At 54.7% college education and $109,090 income, this constituency evaluates candidates primarily on fiscal competence, education policy, and quality-of-life governance — not on cultural grievance or identity-based messaging. Candidates of either party who lead with cultural or identity-based positioning in a district this affluent and educated will be perceived as missing the actual concerns of voters who have the sophistication and economic security to evaluate on material governance. This is a district that rewards concrete policy positioning and punishes culture-war rhetoric as a substitute for substantive answers." }
