        issues: [
          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 15% college-educated and $56,708 median income, MI-HD-26 is a working-class district where access to better jobs, skills training, and wage growth determine whether voters believe government is improving their material conditions. Vocational and technical education, employer partnerships, and apprenticeship pathways are not abstract workforce policy — they are direct constituent needs for households where education attainment is low and income growth depends on access to skilled employment. A representative who engages these concerns with specificity, naming local employers and describing concrete training access, demonstrates investment in the daily economic reality of working people." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Economic Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 35.7% renters and 37.1 median age, this district has a substantial younger adult and renting constituency facing housing cost pressure and economic instability that differs materially from homeowner concerns. Eviction prevention, rental assistance, accessible credit for first-time renters, and housing development that serves working-class wage scales are the governance issues that renters evaluate when deciding whether government is serving them. This constituency is large enough to shift electoral outcomes if mobilized around housing insecurity, and specific policy commitments to renter protection and affordable housing access build a durable majority among younger adults." },
          { name: "Multiracial Working-Class Economic Coalition", tag: "careful", why: "The district is 38% Black, 49% white, and 4% Hispanic — a genuinely multiracial working-class community where economic concerns cut across racial lines, but where individual communities can also have distinct policy priorities around healthcare access, criminal justice, and community investment. The 66% Democratic margin reflects multiracial coalition-building, not dominance by any single demographic group. Messaging that addresses economic mobility as a community-wide issue while remaining attentive to the specific health, justice, and investment concerns of Black and Hispanic communities separately maintains the coalition without treating either as a monolithic bloc." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Safe Majority Rather Than a Contested Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result in a district with low education attainment, below-average income, and 35.7% renters is strong but fragile if the incumbent assumes it reflects inherited Democratic loyalty rather than active constituent satisfaction. Working-class voters of all races evaluate based on material economic conditions — whether their wages are rising, whether they can afford housing, whether jobs are accessible — and will shift if they conclude government is not delivering. A representative who consolidates the 66% by messaging social issues while ignoring economic mobility will find that margin compressed when opposition candidates make the economic case credibly." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 34% Opposition as Immovable Conservative Ideology", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-class district at $56,708 income with 35.7% renters, the 34% Republican opposition likely includes white working-class voters frustrated with economic stagnation, younger renters skeptical of government effectiveness, and households where local economic conditions have not improved. These voters are not ideologically conservative on economic issues — they are economically anxious and unconvinced that the incumbent is addressing their material concerns. Dismissing this opposition as immovable ideology rather than engaging their specific economic grievances leaves electoral opportunity on the table and risks further erosion of the 66% in the next cycle." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "A Majority-Black District (38.32%) with Strong Democratic Performance (66%) but Below-State-Average Education and Income Requiring Targeted Economic Mobility Messaging",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MI-HD-26 is a Democratic-held seat with a decisive 66-34 margin, but the district's demographic profile reveals an economically constrained constituency where education and income lag state averages. At $56,708 median household income and only 15% college-educated adults, this is a working-class community where economic mobility, job access, and wage growth are material concerns that determine whether voters feel government is delivering for them. The 38.32% Black population makes this a plurality-Black district — the largest demographic group — but at 49.91% white and 4.19% Hispanic, this is a genuinely multiracial community rather than a monolithic bloc. A representative who consolidates the 66% result by assuming inherited Democratic loyalty without actively addressing the economic pressures facing working-class households across all demographics will find that margin compressed in the next competitive cycle. The 37.1 median age and 35.7% renter rate further signal a constituency with limited asset accumulation and ongoing housing cost pressure — younger adults and renters evaluating whether their economic circumstances are improving or stagnating.",
          "Wegela's 66% victory establishes a strong baseline, but the district's low education attainment (15%) and below-average income ($56,708) present both a governance opportunity and a latent electoral vulnerability. This is not a district where Democratic voters are consolidating around cultural or identity issues primarily — it is a district where working-class households, renters, and younger adults are asking whether the state government is helping them access better jobs, affordable housing, and economic opportunity. The combination of low college attainment and economic constraint means that workforce development, vocational training, and pathways to skilled employment are not abstract policy issues but direct constituent needs that touch the daily calculations of people deciding whether to stay in the district or seek opportunity elsewhere. A representative who engages these concerns with specificity — naming employers, discussing training programs, addressing housing affordability — builds a durable majority by demonstrating that government is invested in the material conditions of working people's lives. Conversely, a representative who defaults to messaging around social issues or abstract Democratic values while ignoring economic mobility will find that the 66% is a ceiling, not a floor."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with economic mobility and workforce development: the 15% college attainment and $56,708 income signal a constituency asking whether the state is helping them access better jobs, training, and opportunity — name specific employers, training programs, and wage-growth initiatives that show government is invested in working people's advancement.",
          "Address the renter and younger adult constituency directly: 35.7% renters at 37.1 median age face housing affordability and economic stability concerns that differ from homeowners — housing cost pressure, eviction prevention, and accessible credit are material issues that renters evaluate before partisan loyalty.",
          "Serve the multiracial working-class base with integrated messaging: this is not a Black district with minority appendages; it is a 38% Black, 49% white, 4% Hispanic working-class community where economic concerns cut across racial lines — frame economic mobility, job access, and housing affordability as community-wide issues, not race-specific ones.",
          "Understand what compressed the opposition to 34%: a 66% Democratic result in a working-class district with low education and economic constraint is strong but not overwhelming — recognize that the 34% Republican opposition likely includes white working-class voters, renters frustrated with economic stagnation, and younger adults skeptical of government effectiveness, and address their concerns directly rather than assuming they are immovable."
        ]
