        issues: [
          { name: "Middle-Class Economic Security & Cost of Living", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $76,232 median household income with a 41.3-year median age and 75.6% homeownership, HD-28 is a homeowner-dominant, middle-income community where property taxes, healthcare costs, and wage stagnation are direct household budget concerns. The 54% Republican margin reflects this constituency's traditional alignment, but with Democratic opposition at 46% — only 8 points down — this is not a safe seat for an incumbent who ignores the material economic pressures of homeowning families. A representative who leads with concrete answers on property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, and local cost-of-living stability consolidates the Republican base; one who doesn't opens space for a credible challenger to reframe the race around pocketbook issues." },
          { name: "Suburban Service Delivery & Local Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "Livonia and the greater Detroit suburban ring depend on municipal services — schools, public safety, road maintenance, water infrastructure — that directly affect property values and quality of life for the 75.6% homeowner majority. The 24.4% renter rate signals a meaningful rental community whose housing stability and service access are also governance priorities. At the state legislative level, local revenue sharing, education funding, and infrastructure investment are the policy levers that determine whether suburban communities can maintain service levels and property tax stability. A representative who engages the local government association, school districts, and municipal leaders with specificity on state-local partnership demonstrates the constituent attention that a suburban district expects." },
          { name: "Community Demographics & Minority Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "With 8.39% Black, 7.07% Hispanic, and 3.08% Asian residents, HD-28 has meaningful minority communities whose economic mobility, healthcare access, and education concerns are constituent priorities that can become invisible if the representation narrative focuses exclusively on the 76.57% white majority. At 54-46, the margin is close enough that inattention to minority community concerns — particularly around education quality, business opportunity, and healthcare access — represents both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability. A representative who actively engages these communities as constituents, not token acknowledgments, demonstrates the inclusive governing posture that the district's actual demographics demand." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Dominant Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 margin in any district is a working majority, not a landslide — and in a suburban Detroit district at $76,232 income where homeowners are acutely aware of rising costs, it is especially precarious. An incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though the current partisan lean is a stable floor, rather than a plurality to be actively defended against economic messaging from the opposition, will find that messaging effective. The 46% opposition is entirely capable of winning this seat if they can build a credible economic narrative and the incumbent becomes invisible to constituent concerns." },
          { name: "Positioning Suburban Community as Inherently Republican", tag: "avoid", why: "Suburban Detroit voters — particularly homeowners and families — are fundamentally pragmatic and will cross partisan lines for candidates they believe will deliver on schools, roads, public safety, and cost-of-living stability. Assuming that 76% white, 75% homeowner, $76k income automatically equals Republican votes is strategically lazy and empirically wrong; the 46% Democratic opposition in this constituency reflects voters who are persuadable on the basis of performance and local governance delivery. A representative who campaigns as though the suburban community's Republican affiliation is fixed rather than conditional on actual service delivery will be surprised by how quickly that 54% can erode." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Suburban Detroit's Pragmatic Homeowners: A 54-46 Middle-Class Seat Where Cost-of-Living and Service Delivery Govern Partisan Loyalty",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "HD-28 is a classic suburban Detroit district: 76.6% white, 75.6% homeowner, $76,232 median income, 41.3-year median age, and a 54-46 Republican margin that, in the context of a suburban community with genuine economic pressures on middle-class families, is more competitive than the headline number suggests. This is not a deeply red district by national or even Michigan standards — it is a working-class-to-upper-middle-class suburban community whose voters evaluate primarily on whether government is delivering for them: schools that work, property taxes they can manage, roads that are maintained, and public safety that is reliable. The 46% Democratic opposition is not a fringe coalition; it is a substantial minority in a community where homeowners are acutely aware of rising property taxes, healthcare costs, and wage stagnation relative to cost-of-living increases. Any representative who wants to hold or win this seat must engage these material concerns with specificity and demonstrate that their legislative agenda is oriented toward addressing the actual budget pressures that define life for a 41-year-old family with a $76k income, a mortgage, and school-age children.",
          "The district's 23.4% college attainment rate, while below state average, signals a community where higher education serves a functional purpose — access to better-paying jobs for young adults and a pathway to middle-class stability — rather than an end in itself. The 24.4% renter rate, though a minority, is large enough to represent a meaningful constituency of younger adults or service-sector workers who are not homeowners and whose concerns around housing stability and affordability are distinct from the homeowner majority's property-tax concerns. With 8.39% Black, 7.07% Hispanic, and 3.08% Asian residents, the district also includes minority communities whose economic mobility, education quality, and healthcare access are constituent priorities. A representative who focuses exclusively on the homeowner majority's tax concerns while neglecting the renter and minority communities' economic security concerns is governing incompletely — and in a district at 54-46, that incompleteness is a strategic vulnerability that a credible opposition can exploit."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with middle-class economic security: property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, school funding stability, and infrastructure maintenance are the material governance concerns that determine whether a homeowner-majority, $76k-income suburban community feels represented or abandoned.",
          "Engage local government and school leadership as institutional partners; suburban voters evaluate state representatives partly on whether they support the municipal and education systems their property taxes fund, and a legislator who builds strong relationships with local governments demonstrates institutional competence.",
          "Recognize the 46% opposition as a real constituency, not a fringe: understand whether it concentrates around specific governance failures, economic frustration, or minority community concerns — and address each with concrete policy responses rather than assuming partisan loyalty will hold.",
          "Serve minority communities actively: with 8.39% Black, 7.07% Hispanic, and 3.08% Asian residents, this district has meaningful communities whose education, economic opportunity, and healthcare concerns are constituent priorities that deserve specific attention, not token acknowledgment."
        ]
