          "The strategic vulnerability in MI-HD-30 is not the Democratic opposition — at 35%, it is structurally limited in a district this demographically homogeneous and this Republican-aligned. The vulnerability, instead, is the gap between the district's modest income ($75,449 median household), aging population (43.2 median age), and below-average college attainment (23.6%), on one hand, and the possibility that an incumbent becomes perceived as inattentive to the material pressures those demographics create. Older homeowners on fixed or slow-growing incomes face rising property taxes, healthcare costs, and utility expenses in ways that abstract conservative messaging does not address. Young adults trying to enter the housing market in a district where homeownership is the norm face affordability constraints that affect whether they stay or leave. A representative who treats 65% as a durable base and campaigns primarily to consolidate Republican voters rather than actively addressing why 35% voted differently — and whether any of those voters represent dissatisfied Republicans — risks discovering that the margin is less stable than it appears. The Democratic 35% in a district this white and homeowner-heavy likely includes not just traditional Democrats but also Republicans frustrated with specific incumbent performance on local issues."
