          { name: "White Working-Class Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 67.81% white, 28.2% renter rate, $64,430 income, and 44.8 median age, MI-HD-38 is a district of working-age homeowners for whom property taxes, home maintenance costs, and healthcare affordability are direct household budget concerns. Andrews' 53% suggests these homeowners are not uniformly consolidated—some portion voted for the opposition. A candidate who leads with specific, material solutions to homeowner cost pressures and property tax fairness builds the white working-class plurality that is essential to winning or holding this seat." },
          { name: "Black Community Service & Representation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 18.82% Black population represents approximately 10-12 percentage points of a 53-47 margin, making Black voter consolidation arithmetically essential to any winning coalition. At $64,430 district income, this community is economically positioned alongside white working-class neighbors, but likely faces distinct barriers in healthcare access, workforce opportunity, and neighborhood investment. Any incumbent or challenger who neglects specific Black community agenda items—education, economic development, healthcare equity—loses a constituency whose votes are mathematically necessary and whose turnout is not automatic." },
          { name: "Unknown Demographic Gap & Turnout Behavior", tag: "careful", why: "The ~5.24% gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% represents 1,500-2,000 voters whose demographic identity and turnout patterns are unknown. In a district decided by 6 points, this gap is large enough to swing the election if it represents a cohesive constituency (American Indian/Alaska Native, additional Hispanic, or immigrant communities) with differential turnout or candidate preference. Any candidate operating without understanding who comprises this 5.24% is flying blind on a constituency that could determine the outcome." },
          { name: "Treating a 53% Result as a Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 margin in a swing state is not a secure majority—it is a narrow plurality in which the incumbent has lost 47% of voters and holds an advantage of only 6 percentage points. Andrews' victory required simultaneous consolidation of white homeowners, Black voters, renters, and the unaccounted-for 5.24%. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though this represents a stable base, rather than a contested coalition requiring constant engagement and delivery, will find this seat flipped in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Ignoring Renter Constituency Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 28.2% renter rate, MI-HD-38 has a renter-plurality younger adult population for whom housing affordability, eviction protection, and tenant rights are direct material concerns. These voters likely evaluate differently than homeowners on property tax issues and home-owner equity concerns. Any candidate whose housing policy defaults to homeowner-centric framing—property tax cuts, home-repair incentives—without addressing renter affordability and tenant protection will lose a constituency that, at nearly 30% of the district, is large enough to swing a 6-point margin." }
