          { name: "Native American Community Engagement and Tribal Governance", tag: "careful", why: "The 5.4% demographic gap not accounted for in the four reported Census categories represents American Indian residents—likely connected to Michigan tribal nations or local Native American communities—whose governance and economic development concerns deserve specific constituent attention rather than invisibility. While this population is not large enough to swing the 63-37 margin, it is present and represents a potential opposition base; a candidate who treats tribal communities as an afterthought rather than a legitimate constituent group risks enabling mobilization if a credible challenger offers authentic engagement. Active, respectful engagement with tribal governments and Native American constituents around economic development, healthcare access, and sovereignty issues builds goodwill and prevents a latent opposition base from consolidating." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "While 63% is a comfortable margin, it is built on consolidation of an Anglo homeowner base in a region experiencing long-term demographic erosion and economic stress—conditions that can shift relatively quickly if household economic anxiety intensifies or if a challenger offers more credible engagement with the constituency's material concerns. The 18.8% renter rate and below-average college attainment indicate a constituency with constrained economic mobility and rising fixed costs, not a demographic base with expanding prosperity or confidence in current trajectory." },
          { name: "Positioning Healthcare or Tax Policy as Abstract Conservative Principle Rather Than Constituent Economic Security", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median income is $67,420 and median age is 40.4, healthcare costs and property tax burden are not abstract fiscal policy questions—they are material household budget constraints that families experience monthly. Any candidate who argues for healthcare or tax policy purely on ideological grounds without demonstrating specific understanding of how those policies affect MI-HD-39 households' actual economic security will communicate a lack of engagement with constituent reality, creating an opening for an opponent with more specific constituent focus." }
