          { name: "Assuming Educational Attainment Automatically Predicts Progressive Politics", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban voters are not a monolithic political constituency, and 50.7% college attainment does not automatically mean this district is inherently Democratic or that education is the sole driver of the 57% margin. Many college-educated, higher-income households evaluate on fiscal conservatism, business climate, or public safety — not educational progressivism. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though college education is synonymous with Democratic allegiance misses the actual heterogeneity of the constituency and fails to engage voters whose values may be complicated and context-dependent rather than ideologically coherent." },
          { name: "Treating Renters as a Separate Constituency from Homeowners", tag: "avoid", why: "With 32.1% renters and 67.9% homeowners, MI-HD-40 is not a renter-dominant district — it is a mixed-tenure community where renters and homeowners coexist economically. Framing housing policy, zoning, or property tax issues as conflicts between renters and homeowners creates a false binary and alienates whichever group feels positioned as the 'opponent.' The actual issue is whether development and housing policy in this suburban district serves both renters' access and homeowners' asset stability — a governance challenge that requires integration, not conflict rhetoric." }
