          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "College-educated adults represent only 23.1% of HD-43, but this is the constituency most responsive to Democratic messaging on education policy, environmental regulation, and expertise-driven governance. In affluent Michigan districts, college-educated professionals have demonstrated willingness to cross partisan lines when they perceive incumbent misalignment with their policy priorities. Sustained engagement with this group—through specific commitments on education funding, environmental stewardship, or local planning—consolidates the Republican base and neutralizes a natural swing constituency." },
          { name: "Non-College Affluent Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 76.9% of constituents without four-year degrees includes successful business owners, tradespeople, and entrepreneurs whose economic security depends on regulatory predictability and local economic dynamism rather than credential status. These voters prioritize growth, low regulatory burden, and tax fairness. An incumbent who understands and articulates this constituency's distinct interests—separate from national Republican messaging—builds a durable base. A challenger who frames policy through workforce opportunity and small-business viability can fracture this group if the incumbent is perceived as misaligned with their economic interests." },
          { name: "Local Governance Credibility & School Funding", tag: "careful", why: "In an affluent homeowner-dominant district where 88.3% own homes, local education quality and property tax fairness are not abstract policy concerns—they directly impact family wealth and quality of life. A 72% result is sustainable only if the incumbent maintains visible, active engagement on school funding adequacy, education quality metrics, and tax burden management. Any accumulated grievance on these issues—whether from professional parents skeptical of curriculum choices or from business owners frustrated with tax burden—can erode the margin faster than national messaging because it strikes at the core reason this constituency votes Republican." },
          { name: "Treating 72% as a Durable Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72% result in an affluent, homeowner-dominant, low-renter district is expected Republican performance, not a resilient supermajority. The absence of demographic diversity (88.8% white, 3.2% unaccounted gap) means there are no hidden Republican constituencies to mobilize further; growth in the margin would require persuasion of existing Democratic voters or college-educated professionals—both strategically costly. More importantly, a 72% result leaves no margin for error on local governance, tax burden, or school quality. An incumbent who campaigns as though this is a safe seat and neglects constituent engagement on these local priorities invites credible challenge." },
          { name: "Assuming Non-College Voters Are Not Persuadable on Economic Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "The 76.9% of constituents without four-year degrees are affluent and economically successful; they are not a monolithic bloc. A Democratic challenger who frames economic policy around infrastructure investment, healthcare cost control, or union-based wage protection can gain meaningful traction with non-college small-business owners and tradespeople if they perceive the incumbent as economically misaligned. Treating this group as locked-in Republican votes rather than as persuadable on their specific economic interests is strategically naive and creates an opening for a competent challenger." }
