          { name: "Housing Affordability and Tenant Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 47.6% renters, MI-HD-53 has the highest renter concentration in this analysis and one of the lowest median incomes at $52,652. For a renter household at or below this income, housing cost burden is typically catastrophic — consuming 40-50%+ of household income. Tenant protection laws, rent stabilization frameworks, landlord accountability mechanisms, and enforcement of habitability standards are not ideological positions but direct household budget policy. A representative who leads on housing policy addresses the material economic concern that defines a near-majority of the district's households." },
          { name: "Racial Equity in Healthcare, Criminal Justice, and Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The African American plurality at 33.89% is the single largest constituency bloc and the demographic foundation of the 66% Democratic margin. This community has material stakes in healthcare access disparities, criminal justice policy, hiring discrimination, and housing equity that go beyond symbolic representation. Legislative specificity on these issues — Medicaid expansion, police accountability, fair housing enforcement — is what translates electoral alignment into constituents' lived experiences of being represented. Generic appeals to community identity without concrete legislative agenda will be perceived as hollow by a constituency evaluating whether their representative is actually delivering on the material conditions of their lives." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Visibility and Workplace/Immigration Policy", tag: "careful", why: "At 16.7%, the Hispanic population is large enough to be outcome-determinative in a competitive race but small enough to become invisible in Black-white demographic framings. In a district where media, legislative attention, and campaign focus default to African American issues, Hispanic voters' specific concerns around workplace protections, immigration policy, language access in public services, and economic opportunity can fall into a gap where they are nominally included but not substantively engaged. In a 66-34 district, this matters less; in a close race, this is how challengers win by consolidating secondary constituencies that feel unheard." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Safe Margin That Permits Neglect of Constituent Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% Democratic margin in an urban, majority-minority district with high renter concentration and low median income reflects genuine constituent alignment on material economic concerns, not inherited loyalty that survives inattention. If a representative consolidates only the existing Democratic base without actively addressing the housing affordability, healthcare, and economic mobility concerns that drive that alignment, a challenger who engages these issues with greater specificity and urgency can erode the margin significantly. Urban constituencies have options and are willing to shift if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering." },
          { name: "Framing Tenant Protection or Housing Policy as Ideologically Divisive Rather Than Economic", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 47.6% are renters at $52,652 median income, tenant protection and housing affordability are not ideological positions — they are direct household economic policy. Framing these as left-wing activism or big-government overreach rather than as solutions to a material economic problem (housing cost burden exceeding 40% of household income) will be perceived as tone-deaf by a constituency for whom housing is an active financial crisis, not an abstract policy debate. This framing opens space for a challenger to present themselves as more responsive to actual household needs." }
