        issues: [
          { name: "High-Income, College-Educated Professional Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "MI-HD-56 is one of the most affluent and educationally advanced districts in the state, with a $109,874 median household income and 63.6% college attainment. This constituency evaluates candidates on competence, policy specificity, and alignment with professional-class economic interests — not partisan loyalty alone. A 57% Democratic margin in what should demographically be a swing district reflects active engagement of this educated professional base by the incumbent, and any challenger must offer a credible alternative that speaks to this constituency's actual priorities: tax policy, education quality, healthcare access, and economic growth." },
          { name: "Asian American Community Engagement & Growth", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 21.71%, the Asian American population is the second-largest demographic group in MI-HD-56 and represents one of the most politically engaged and growing constituencies in the district. This population is concentrated in professional and entrepreneurial sectors, has high educational attainment, and participates actively in civic life. The incumbent's 57% result was built partly on authentic engagement with this community around economic opportunity, immigration policy nuance, and professional-class concerns. Any challenger must recognize that the Asian American community is not a minority to be managed peripherally but a core constituency whose support determines the election outcome." },
          { name: "Suburban Growth, Density, and Quality-of-Life Priorities", tag: "careful", why: "The 40.7-year median age, 27.3% renter rate, and $109,874 income profile suggest a suburban constituency mixing established homeowners with younger professionals and families in rental housing. This mixed-tenure community has competing priorities: homeowners focus on property values and tax stability, renters on housing affordability and neighborhood investment. At 57%, the Democratic margin may reflect renter constituency strength or specific housing policy alignment. A challenger must understand whether the opposition is concentrated among renters frustrated by housing costs or among homeowners concerned about density and change — and offer concrete answers that don't require choosing between these constituencies." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safely Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57% result in a 63.6% college-educated, $109,874-income district is a working margin, not a landslide. This is a highly educated, professionally engaged constituency that evaluates on specific policy performance and candidate competence. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 57% is a durable floor without actively engaging the college-educated professional base on current economic and policy concerns risks erosion to a credible challenger who offers a more compelling professional-class alternative." },
          { name: "Ignoring or Tokenizing the Asian American Community", tag: "avoid", why: "At 21.71%, the Asian American population is too large and too politically active to be addressed through generic minority outreach or symbolic gestures. This community has high civic participation, specific policy concerns around immigration, education, entrepreneurship, and professional advancement, and direct experience evaluating candidates on whether they take these concerns seriously. Any candidate whose engagement with the Asian American community reads as superficial or instrumental — rather than rooted in genuine understanding of the community's economic interests and civic priorities — will find that inauthentic approach actively costly in a district where this population is a key swing constituency." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Michigan's Most Affluent, Highly Educated Suburban District: 57% Democratic on Professional-Class Alignment and Active Asian American Engagement",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MI-HD-56 is one of Michigan's most economically advantaged and educationally sophisticated districts, characterized by a $109,874 median household income — well above state average — and 63.6% college attainment among adults. The demographic profile is fundamentally suburban and professional: 66.67% white, 21.71% Asian American, and a 40.7-year median age reflecting an established but still-growing community mixing long-term homeowners with younger professionals and families. The 57% Democratic result that Macdonell achieved is not explained by typical partisan demographics — this should be a competitive or even slightly Republican-leaning district on income and education alone. The actual result reflects active Democratic engagement of the college-educated professional constituency and specific community-building work with the Asian American population, which at nearly 22% of the district is the second-largest demographic group and a critical swing constituency. Any candidate who wants to hold or win this seat must recognize that it is won or lost on the specific policy competence and professional-class alignment that a $109,874-income, 63.6%-college-educated constituency demands.",
          "The district's tenure and housing mix — 72.7% homeowners, 27.3% renters — creates competing priorities that determine electoral outcomes. Established homeowners prioritize property values, tax stability, school quality, and neighborhood character; renters prioritize housing affordability, walkability, and neighborhood investment. The 57% Democratic margin may reflect renter constituency strength, homeowner alignment on education funding or local governance, or both — but the key strategic fact is that this is not a cohesive demographic bloc. The incumbent's 57% was built on coalition-building across these tenure groups and on credible engagement with the Asian American professional community around specific issues: entrepreneurship support, immigration policy sophistication, education quality, and economic opportunity. A challenger who wants to compete must either consolidate the college-educated professional base more effectively than the incumbent or build a genuinely alternative coalition that addresses specific constituency concerns the incumbent is underserving. At 57%, neither consolidation of the existing Republican base nor generic appeals to homeowner values will be sufficient — the challenger must win this seat by offering specific policy answers to what this highly educated, economically secure, and civically engaged constituency actually needs."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with professional-class competence and policy specificity: the $109,874-income, 63.6%-college-educated constituency does not respond to partisan messaging or ideological positioning — it evaluates on whether the candidate understands current policy challenges in education, taxation, economic development, and healthcare with actual nuance and offers credible solutions.",
          "Engage the Asian American community as a core constituency, not a minority group: at 21.71%, this population is large enough and politically active enough to determine the election outcome; authentic engagement requires specific knowledge of community economic interests, immigration policy, education priorities, and professional advancement concerns — not generic multicultural outreach.",
          "Develop a clear housing and neighborhood strategy that addresses both homeowner stability and renter affordability without forcing these constituencies to choose: the 27.3% renter rate and 72.7% homeowner split are both significant, and any candidate who favors one group at the other's expense loses the middle of this election.",
          "Understand specifically what drove the 43% opposition in a district this affluent and educated: whether it is concentrated among homeowners frustrated by property taxes, among renters priced out of housing, or among Asian American professionals who perceive insufficient representation — and offer concrete policy answers rather than assuming partisan loyalty will hold."
        ]
