          { name: "Relying on Partisan Registration Alone in a College-Educated District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57% Republican margin in a district with 37.6% college attainment and $83,849 median income is not a stable floor; educated, affluent voters in suburban districts increasingly evaluate candidates on governance competence, institutional integrity, and quality-of-life delivery rather than partisan habit. Any candidate who assumes the 57% is secure and campaigns primarily to the existing Republican base without actively engaging the 43% opposition and the college-educated swing voters within the Republican base will find that margin erode in a competitive cycle or lose persuadable voters to a credible challenger offering better governance messaging." },
          { name: "Treating 43% Opposition as Marginal or Ignorable in a Suburban Context", tag: "avoid", why: "The 43% Democratic share in MI-HD-57 is not a fringe constituency; in a district this prosperous and educated, it represents a substantial bloc of affluent, college-educated homeowners who have actively chosen an alternative to the Republican candidate. This is a professional class with the resources and sophistication to organize, to communicate grievances, and to support a credible challenger; treating them as background noise or assuming they lack the capacity to shift the outcome is a strategic error that compounds complacency about education quality, fiscal management, and community engagement." }
