          { name: "College-Educated Voter Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 30.1% college attainment, MI-HD-58 is significantly above the national rural average and well-positioned within suburban Michigan. This educated cohort — particularly among younger professionals — evaluates candidates on issue specificity, evidence-based governance, and responsiveness to quality-of-life concerns like schools, water quality, and local infrastructure investment. The 47% opposition in a college-educated-heavy district suggests that Robinson's coalition does not fully capture this constituency's votes; candidates who demonstrate substantive competence on education funding, environmental stewardship, and smart growth will find receptive voters here." },
          { name: "Renter and Younger Adult Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "At 32.4% renters, MI-HD-58 has a sizable non-homeowner constituency whose economic priorities differ sharply from the homeowner majority — affordability, access to reliable transit, job market strength, and whether local wages are rising with cost of living are the material concerns of renters in a district where median income is $74,260. The 47% opposition could be concentrated here; candidates must distinguish between homeowner and renter economic needs rather than treating the district as a monolithic middle-class unit. Renters' concerns about whether the community is affordable for young families and workers are real constituent issues that deserve explicit campaign attention." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 margin in a suburban Michigan district with 30.1% college attainment, 32.4% renters, and $74,260 median income is not a durable base — it is a contested constituency where educational attainment and economic diversity mean voters have real alternatives. Robinson's 2020 win did not produce a coalition so consolidated that it can absorb challenger quality without response; any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 53% represents a secure hold will find that assumption tested quickly by a credible alternative who engages the college-educated and renter constituencies directly." },
          { name: "Polarizing Rhetoric or Treating Constituent Diversity as Political Liability", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 30.1% college education, meaningful racial diversity (77.73% white with 8.49% Black and 6.1% Asian residents), and competitive partisan alignment does not respond to divisive or inflammatory messaging — it responds to candidates who can articulate a coherent vision for shared prosperity that acknowledges and serves the district's actual diversity. Any candidate whose campaign relies on culture-war framing or treats the college-educated or minority constituencies as out-of-district special interests will find these voters actively mobilizing against that candidacy. In MI-HD-58, demographic diversity is a stable feature; governing with that reality in mind is an electoral requirement, not an option." }
