          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $103,881 median income with a 45.2 median age, this is a constituency actively engaged with healthcare decisions — aging parents, family coverage, prescription costs, and Medicare planning are material concerns. College-educated professionals in this income bracket have demonstrated willingness to defect from Republican voting patterns on healthcare policy when they perceive inadequate access or cost management. The incumbent's record on Medicaid, rural hospital viability, and prescription drug cost policy is a direct measure of whether this constituency remains consolidated." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Volatility", tag: "careful", why: "National electoral trends show college-educated suburban professionals increasingly volatile in their partisan alignment, particularly in districts where education and healthcare policy shape voting more than cultural conservatism. At 39.4% college attainment in a $103,881 income district, MI-HD-59 contains a professional constituency that has elsewhere shifted toward Democratic candidates when they perceive Republican governance as inadequate on education or healthcare. The 31% Democratic share may represent a floor that expands if Democratic messaging on these issues proves credible and the incumbent's record appears weak." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 margin in a district with high income, high education, and high homeownership is deceptively large — the demographic profile does not obviously predict such a lopsided result, suggesting the margin reflects alignment between the incumbent's personal profile and this specific constituency rather than inevitable partisan loyalty. College-educated professionals have demonstrated in other districts that they will defect when they perceive governance failure on education or healthcare, and any incumbent who treats 69% as a floor rather than a result that must be actively defended risks finding the margin compressed in a competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Neglecting Policy Substance in Favor of Cultural Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency this affluent and educated — $103,881 income, 39.4% college attainment, 45.2 median age — evaluates primarily on policy competence and governance effectiveness, not cultural conservatism or partisan identity. Any candidate who leads on abstract conservative cultural messaging while offering vague positions on education funding, healthcare access, or fiscal management misreads what actually moves this electorate and creates an opening for a challenger with stronger policy substance on the issues that matter to suburban professionals." }
