          { name: "Treating 62% as a Safe Majority in an Educated, Persuadable District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 result in a high-income, college-educated district does not represent ideological consolidation or a stable partisan floor — it represents a working majority in a constituency where affluent, educated voters regularly evaluate candidates on specific governance performance and material economic concerns. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 62% creates a safe hold, rather than recognizing that educated professionals are consistently persuadable on competence and policy specifics, risks losing the attention of the swing voters whose support produced that margin." },
          { name: "Generic Partisan Positioning Over Specific Governance Solutions", tag: "avoid", why: "With 34.4% college attainment and $99,335 median income, MI-HD-60 is a constituency that punishes generic partisan rhetoric and rewards specific, credible policy solutions on the material concerns of homeowners and professionals. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — who relies on partisan positioning or abstract ideological appeals rather than demonstrating specific understanding of property tax policy, healthcare cost drivers, or infrastructure investment will find this educated, affluent constituency unresponsive. The 38% opposition may include voters genuinely available to a credible alternative; generic messaging will not persuade them." }
