          { name: "Renter Population & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 30.3% renter rate represents approximately 5,000-6,000 households without asset-holding stability, likely skewing younger and more Democratic than the homeowner majority. Housing costs and rental market pressures are the primary economic concerns for this constituency, and they are often invisible in discussions framed around property-tax fairness or homeowner concerns. Specific advocacy on affordable housing development, renter protections, and the wage-to-rent ratios that young adults face can build inroads into this segment without alienating homeowners — and prevents a challenger from monopolizing the renter vote in a district where every percentage point of turnout matters." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & American Indian/Alaska Native Constituent Needs", tag: "careful", why: "The 4.8% gap in reported Census categories likely includes American Indian/Alaska Native residents, whose specific civic and economic concerns around tribal sovereignty, healthcare access, and economic development may not be immediately visible in the baseline political math. In Michigan's northern regions particularly, AIAN constituents can represent both relatively small percentages and genuinely important electoral margins if mobilized around issues that affect them directly. Authentic constituent engagement here prevents unexpected challenger recruitment or coalition-building that activates this population in ways unfavorable to the incumbent." },
          { name: "Treating 56-44 as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result in Michigan's competitive political environment is not a comfortable margin — it reflects 44% of the district actively choosing an alternative, and the straightforward demographics suggest this opposition cannot be explained away as concentrated in a hostile demographic segment. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 56% represents a durable base rather than an active choice by homeowners to support Republican governance will find that active choice withdrawn if governance performance falters or if a challenger makes a credible economic argument to the renter population or younger households." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Over Local Economic Advocacy", tag: "avoid", why: "With 22.4% college attainment and $69,107 median income in a district where 30.3% are renters and housing costs are rising, a campaign centered on abstract conservative positioning rather than concrete local economic advocacy misreads the constituency. This district is evaluating on whether government is delivering for them in practical terms — job creation, healthcare access, tax burden fairness, local service quality — not whether the representative aligns with national partisan identities. A candidate who prioritizes state-level ideological battles over constituent service in a district this economically mixed will find the 44% opposition mobilized by a challenger with better local economic messaging." }
