          "MI-HD-66 is a high-income, college-educated, heavily homeowner district that has delivered a comfortable 68-32 Republican margin—a result that, on its demographic surface, appears straightforward and stable. The $108,819 median household income is among the highest in any district; 40.4% college attainment signals professional and managerial households; 11.3% renter rate reflects an asset-holding, property-invested constituency; and 42.8 median age indicates a settled, established community rather than a young, transient one. These are the demographics of a district where homeownership, educational achievement, and economic stability are the primary drivers of both Republican alignment and electoral behavior. The 68% margin is real, but it is built on consolidation of this affluent, educated, homeowner base—not on persuasion of opposition voters or on deep engagement with constituencies outside this primary coalition.",
          "The strategic vulnerability in MI-HD-66 lies in what the demographic data does not fully capture: the reported Census categories account for 97.08% of the population, leaving approximately a 2.92% gap that likely represents American Indian/Alaska Native, multiracial, or other residents whose presence in the district is real but unquantified. More consequentially, the district's Hispanic/Latino population of 4.57% and Asian population of 1.51%, while numerically small, represent constituencies whose economic concerns—healthcare affordability, housing costs, educational access, and economic mobility—may not align with the messaging that consolidates the 68% Republican base. At $108,819 income and 40.4% college attainment, MI-HD-66 is a district where affluent homeowners dominate, but the 32% Democratic share and the presence of minority communities signal that a representative who speaks only to the economic security of the existing Republican coalition risks allowing those smaller constituencies to organize an alternative if they perceive neglect. The incumbent's strategic task is not to flip opposition voters en masse, but to actively govern in ways that make the case to the 32% opposition and to minority communities that their concerns are taken seriously—and to prevent a future challenger from mobilizing that opposition around specific grievances about representation."
