          { name: "Middle-Class Economic Security and Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $78,224 median household income with 73.4% homeownership, MI-HD-76 is a district of employed adults whose economic security is real but not guaranteed. Rising healthcare costs, housing cost growth, childcare affordability, and property tax increases are material economic anxieties for this demographic, and they directly shape voting behavior in a 52-48 district. Any candidate who responds to these concerns with specific fiscal policy — rather than cultural positioning or abstract economic messaging — addresses the actual decision-making calculus of suburban middle-class voters. The 4% margin suggests that whichever candidate better demonstrates that their governance improves the financial reality of $78K households wins." },
          { name: "Suburban Infrastructure and Service Delivery Credibility", tag: "lean-into", why: "Suburban voters evaluate candidates first on unglamorous governance basics: road maintenance, school funding allocation, public-safety response times, and whether local government is run efficiently and responsively. At 41.2-year median age, most of MI-HD-76 has families in schools or young children in childcare; school quality and responsive local government are direct quality-of-life issues, not peripheral concerns. Witwer's 52% likely reflects demonstrated competence on these basics; any challenger must either show superior execution capacity or identify specific service failures and offer concrete remedies. This is where suburban elections are actually won — not in statewide ideological debates, but in constituent services performance and visible local problem-solving." },
          { name: "The College-Educated vs. Non-College Voter Split", tag: "careful", why: "At 31.2% college attainment, MI-HD-76 has a meaningful college-educated minority (roughly 1 in 3) and a non-college working majority (roughly 2 in 3) whose policy priorities and persuasion triggers differ significantly. College-educated professionals may prioritize education investment and environmental policy; non-college workers focus more on fiscal restraint, small-business viability, and property-tax fairness. The 4% margin suggests that neither constituency alone can produce victory; any candidate who consolidates only the college-educated base or ignores their distinct concerns risks losing the middle-class working plurality. Candidate messaging must acknowledge that these are distinct constituencies with legitimate, different priorities rather than treating 'suburbanites' as monolithic." },
          { name: "Framing a 52% Win as a Stable Democratic Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "Witwer's 52% is mathematically a one-vote margin in a district where 48% chose another direction; treating this as a safe Democratic seat is strategically misreading the district's actual competitive reality. A 4-point margin in a suburban district with a mixed educational profile and a significant non-college working population is not a durable coalition — it is a hard-fought outcome that requires active maintenance of both the constituencies that delivered it and specific responses to the concerns driving the 48% opposition. Any Democratic strategy that treats this seat as safe and shifts energy to more threatening districts risks losing the 52% in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Assuming Democratic Cultural Issues Are Persuasive to Swing Voters", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 52-48 district where the median household income is $78K, median age is 41, and college attainment is 31%, cultural positioning on abortion, gun policy, or LGBTQ+ issues is unlikely to be the primary swing factor — and in fact, leading with these issues in a district with substantial non-college working population risks reinforcing opposition turnout. Middle-class suburbanites in this demographic profile evaluate candidates primarily on fiscal management, service delivery, and whether state policy improves their economic security; cultural debates are secondary to material household concerns. Any candidate who leads with cultural positioning instead of economic competence is ceding the district's decision-making frame to their opponent." }
