          { name: "School Funding & Educational Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "School quality is the dominant economic and social concern for homeowner-majority suburban districts, and with 77.5% white composition and significant affluent population, educational outcomes directly shape property values and family retention. MI HD-81's affluent base prioritizes both school funding adequacy and property tax restraint — requiring legislative competence that balances resource provision with fiscal discipline. A representative must command credible expertise on educational funding mechanisms, state school finance law, and local control pathways in order to claim authority in this constituency." },
          { name: "Working-Class & Minority Community Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "The 6.12% Hispanic and 7.78% Black populations comprise meaningful minority communities whose workforce development, housing affordability, and healthcare access needs differ from the affluent homeowner majority. A 56% Democratic margin in a district this prosperous can erode if the campaign becomes primarily about affluent-focused priorities while these communities perceive their economic concerns as peripheral. Visible commitment to economic mobility pathways for working-class and minority constituents is necessary both as governing substance and as electoral protection against erosion among younger, more diverse voters." },
          { name: "Anti-Tax Messaging Without Fiscal Credibility", tag: "avoid", why: "Republican challengers in affluent suburban districts routinely attack Democratic incumbents on fiscal management using tax-cutting rhetoric that lacks specificity about which services would be cut. A credible challenger in MI HD-81 who can position themselves as fiscally responsible stewards of an affluent community's interests — emphasizing efficient local government and school funding adequacy without tax increases — has direct appeal to the 44% Republican baseline and latent persuadability among swing voters concerned about property tax burden." },
          { name: "Treating a 56% Margin as an Insurmountable Democratic Hold", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56% result in a district this affluent and educated is not overwhelming dominance — it is a working margin held in a constituency that evaluates on governance performance and material outcomes rather than partisan loyalty. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns as though 56% represents a safe majority rather than as a result requiring active re-engagement with an evaluating electorate will find that margin eroded by Republican challenge focused on fiscal management and school quality. The 44% Republican share represents engaged suburban voters with genuine persuadability if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering." }
