          "Engage the college-educated 40.4% on education funding and workforce development: professionals in this income and attainment bracket have high expectations for K-12 and higher education quality, and are responsive to arguments about how education investment drives economic competitiveness and property values — this is the constituency most likely to split Republican while remaining persuadable on specific governance issues.",
          "Understand the 36% opposition specifically: determine whether it is concentrated among younger professionals, urban residents, or specific geographic pockets within the district, and whether it represents firm Democratic voters or college-educated swing voters responsive to alternative candidates and messaging — the difference determines whether the 64% baseline is durable or vulnerable.",
          "Avoid treating demographic homogeneity as a permission to ignore minority communities: while 87.77% white, the 5.82% Hispanic/Latino and 6.5% other populations have specific economic and civic concerns that deserve constituent attention rather than assuming they are safely ignored in a district this demographically dominant — targeted service and visibility can consolidate margins and prevent a challenger from building organizing infrastructure in overlooked communities."
