          { name: "Treating the 70% as a Durable Floor Rather Than a Defensive Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70% result is not an indicator of a safe seat — it is the current manifestation of a coalition that includes a working-class, healthcare-anxious constituency whose loyalty is conditioned on economic security and local governance competence, not inherited partisan identity. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 70% represents a stable majority and who deprioritizes healthcare costs, rural service viability, and constituent service in favor of partisan positioning is creating the conditions for a credible challenger to move the needle 5-7 points and flip the seat." },
          { name: "Rural Community Service Neglect in Favor of Partisan Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 23.4% college attainment and working-class income, constituents evaluate based on whether government is delivering for them materially — not on partisan consistency or ideological purity. An incumbent whose legislative record shows more investment in partisan battles than in local road infrastructure, school funding adequacy, or rural healthcare partnerships is vulnerable to a challenger who offers visible local problem-solving, regardless of partisan alignment. This constituency rewards concrete results over rhetoric." }
