          "Do not treat the 70% result as a permanent floor: in a district with only 23.4% college attainment and working-class income sensitivity, a credible Democratic challenger who mobilizes on healthcare costs and property tax fairness while offering local governance competence can move the needle by 5-7 points, particularly if turnout rises among younger and lower-income households.",
          "Maintain active constituent engagement in smaller communities and townships: the 17.6% renter rate indicates a homeowner-dominant district, but that 17.6% represents renters and younger adults whose economic mobility concerns may not be fully aligned with the incumbent's messaging — regular presence in all communities, not just homeowner-concentrated areas, prevents a challenger from organizing these constituencies into a swing bloc."
