          { name: "Treating 74% as a Durable Mandate Rather Than a Current Margin Dependent on Opposition Weakness", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result in a district this economically stressed (45.9 age, 16.1% college, $59,082 income) reflects opposition party weakness and incumbent name recognition — not affirmative voter enthusiasm or satisfaction with governance outcomes. An incumbent who campaigns the next cycle as though 74% is a stable floor, rather than doubling down on visible constituent service and material problem-solving on healthcare, property taxes, and economic security, risks being vulnerable to a credible challenger who offers specific solutions the incumbent has treated as peripheral." },
          { name: "Framing Opposition as Ideologically Alien Rather Than Engaging Its Actual Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The 26% Democratic share in a deeply red state should not be dismissed as residual partisan affiliation — it likely reflects specific frustrations (healthcare access, wage stagnation, rural service decline) that a challenger with credible solutions could amplify. An incumbent who responds to opposition by framing it as culturally or ideologically foreign to the district, rather than offering material answers to its economic concerns, validates the opposition's framing that representation is not actually solving local problems. This posture risks converting a 74% win into vulnerability when opposition messaging gains specificity." }
