          { name: "Rural Service Delivery & Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 14.8% renter rate and 50.8-year median age, this is a homeowner-majority district whose residents have long-term stakes in community infrastructure—roads, water systems, emergency services, and rural broadband. State policy on rural service funding, infrastructure maintenance, and cost-sharing between rural and urban areas directly affects household quality of life and property values. A representative who delivers on rural infrastructure concerns and maintains active engagement with county and local officials builds trust among a constituency that evaluates largely on whether government is maintaining the community they have invested in." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap & Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for approximately 96% of the population, leaving a ~4% gap likely representing American Indian/Alaska Native, immigrant, or other minority residents whose needs and concerns can become invisible in a district focused on the Anglo majority. While small in percentage terms, this constituency could represent 3,000-5,000 residents in a typical legislative district; their presence requires specific outreach and responsiveness. In a district where the incumbent holds 71%, neglecting even small minority communities creates openings for challengers to mobilize new or deactivated voters around specific issues." },
          { name: "Treating 71% as a Permanent & Uncontestable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 71% margin in any district reflects a particular moment's alignment between an incumbent's profile, the opposing candidate's weakness or unfamiliarity, and current voter sentiment—not a permanent feature of the electorate. In a district with $52,896 median income and only 15.8% college education, the 29% opposition likely reflects material economic concerns, not partisan weakness. Any incumbent who treats 71% as a floor rather than a result-to-maintain risks loss of constituent engagement, opening space for a challenger with a credible economic-security message to rapidly erode the margin." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Messaging Over Constituent-Level Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where educational attainment is only 15.8%, abstract conservative or libertarian positioning resonates less effectively than specific answers to neighbors' concrete problems: how will state policy help me afford healthcare? Will rural roads be maintained? Can I afford to stay in my home as I age? A representative who substitutes partisan talking points for constituent-level problem-solving surrenders the primary advantage of an incumbent—direct engagement with voters' lived experience and material concerns—and creates vulnerability to a challenger who offers specific policy alternatives to voters' real frustrations." }
