          { name: "Household Economic Security for Aging Homeowners", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 45.7 median age, $66,809 income, and only 17% renters, MI-HD-102 is a district of middle-aged homeowners in their peak cost-of-living vulnerability: property taxes, healthcare premiums, home maintenance, and potential long-term care are rising faster than income growth in this income bracket. A representative who demonstrates concrete understanding of these pressures—and legislative action to address them—directly reinforces the incumbent's relationship with the constituency's material concerns. Ignoring household financial stress in favor of abstract ideological positioning leaves voters open to a challenger who speaks to their lived economic reality." },
          { name: "Constituent Service & Healthcare Navigation", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 45.7-year-old constituency with $66,809 median income is actively navigating Medicare transitions, healthcare plan complexity, and aging-parent care coordination—and constituent service infrastructure that helps households navigate these systems builds durable representative-constituent relationships. Active engagement on property tax relief, healthcare appeal processes, and senior service access converts passive 64% support into active loyalty that survives electoral challenges." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap Engagement (AIAN ~4.24%)", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 95.76% of the district, leaving a ~4.24% gap that likely includes American Indian residents. While small in percentage terms, Native American voter mobilization in Michigan has proven capable of shifting margins when engaged around specific issues—tribal healthcare, economic development, and community investment being most relevant. A representative who actively engages this community (if present) rather than assuming the 64% partisan result accounts for their preferences demonstrates governance maturity and protects against unexpected opposition mobilization." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as a Supermajority Requiring No Active Maintenance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 result is a solid but not dominant outcome; one in three voters actively chose an alternative, and that 36% represents real constituent dissatisfaction with something—whether household economic concerns, healthcare access, tax policy, or local governance failures. A representative who campaigns this seat as if the margin is self-sustaining and requires no active engagement with the opposition's driving concerns risks finding the margin eroded by a challenger who speaks more credibly to voters' material vulnerabilities." },
          { name: "Ignoring Household Economic Anxiety in Favor of Partisan Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "The $66,809 income and 45.7 median age signal a constituency navigating real household cost pressures—rising healthcare, property taxes, aging-parent care—that are not solved by partisan rhetoric or abstract ideology. A representative whose legislative record and constituent messaging focus on culture-war or partisan differentiation rather than demonstrating concrete solutions to households' financial pressures will appear disconnected from the constituency's lived reality. In a 64-36 district, that disconnect is an opening for a challenger positioned as more responsive to economic concerns." }
