          { name: "Understanding the 48% Opposition in an Affluent, Educated District", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 52% Democratic result in a district with $82,885 income and 45.5% college education should be winning by a larger margin if demographic alignment is working as intended. The 48% opposition in a high-income, college-educated suburban district represents either specific policy disagreements (healthcare, education, taxes, social issues) or incumbent-specific vulnerabilities that demand diagnostic clarity. Any candidate who fails to identify and directly address what generated nearly half the constituency's opposition is ceding the ability to consolidate or expand beyond the current 52% baseline." },
          { name: "High-Income Homeowner Economic & Fiscal Concerns", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $82,885 median income and an implied high homeownership rate in a 23% renter district, MI-HD-103 is anchored by affluent homeowners whose primary economic concerns likely center on property taxation, healthcare costs, and fiscal responsibility in local government. The incumbent's record on tax policy, school funding sources, and healthcare access for this affluent but not ultra-wealthy constituency is directly relevant to holding the 52%; a challenger who articulates credible fiscal alternatives has a direct opening to the 48% opposition." },
          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Voters' Policy Priorities", tag: "careful", why: "With 45.5% college attainment, MI-HD-103 is home to a significantly educated constituency whose policy priorities often diverge from both partisan stereotypes and the broader electorate—education quality, social institutional stability, healthcare access, and environmental issues are common focus areas. The incumbent's alignment with this constituency's specific policy priorities on these dimensions is a critical retention factor; where the incumbent has diverged from educated suburban preferences, the challenger has an opening to a constituency that should be naturally aligned with the Democrat but is not." },
          { name: "Taking a 52% Result as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result in any district is a plurality of contested voters, not a stable majority, and in a high-income, college-educated suburban district where demographic alignment should produce a larger Democratic margin, the 4-point result signals genuine competitive vulnerability. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 52% represents a comfortable hold rather than an urgent signal to consolidate and expand will find themselves vulnerable to a credible challenger who engages the opposition directly." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Favorability Alone Provides Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "The demographic profile—affluent, educated, older, predominantly white—appears structurally favorable to the incumbent, but the 52% result proves that demographic favorability has not translated into durable electoral security. Over-relying on demographic alignment while ignoring the specific policy concerns that generated the 48% opposition inverts the analytical priority and guarantees vulnerability when a challenger articulates a credible alternative on the issues that matter most to this constituency." }
