          { name: "Conflating Racial Diversity with Political Homogeneity", tag: "avoid", why: "The district's 41.83% Black, 40.28% white, 10.7% Asian, and 2.22% Hispanic composition — plus 5.5% unaccounted for — is genuinely diverse, but diversity does not equal uniform political alignment. Black voters, white voters, and Asian American voters each contain internal variance on economic policy, criminal justice, education, and social issues; treating 'communities of color' as a single voting bloc obscures the specific economic and policy concerns that drive actual electoral behavior. The 26.6% college attainment and $48,173 income suggest a constituency where economic anxiety is high across racial lines, and candidates who address material economic concerns with specificity rather than identity-focused rhetoric tend to consolidate support more effectively." }
