          { name: "College-Educated Voter Consolidation & Professional Economy", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 24.3% college attainment—above Michigan's state average—MI-SD-4 contains a meaningful professional and educated workforce constituency whose voting patterns have shifted Democratic in recent cycles but remain persuadable on specific issues. The suburban geography and $75k+ income suggest professionals in healthcare, education, tech, and management sectors who value quality schools, institutional stability, and competent governance. For a Democratic incumbent at 55%, this educated middle-class base is essential to hold; any candidate who cannot speak fluently about education funding, workforce development, and the economic return on public investment risks losing the voters most likely to punish underperformance." },
          { name: "Black Community Voter Mobilization & Support", tag: "careful", why: "The 13.58% Black population represents roughly one-seventh of the district, making it a significant Democratic-aligned constituency whose turnout variation can shift a 55-45 race materially. At a 10-point Democratic margin, Black voter turnout and enthusiasm for the Democratic incumbent directly correlates to whether that margin holds or contracts in the next cycle. The incumbent won at 55%; if Black turnout or support softens even modestly—dropping from 88% to 83% support, or from 65% to 58% turnout—the margin collapses into true vulnerability. This is not a constituency to take for granted, but one requiring active constituent service, visible investment in community priorities, and clear articulation of how Democratic governance delivers material benefits." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Safe Democratic Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result in a suburban district at a moment of economic anxiety and political flux is not a durable majority—it is a working majority held by consolidating swing voters and base turnout in the same cycle. In a state where Republican structural advantages are significant and suburban demographics have been volatile, 55% represents the margin an incumbent must hold through active constituent service and specific policy delivery, not a floor below which the seat is safe. Any Democratic incumbent or challenger who treats this seat as though 55% is a comfortable cushion rather than a narrow mandate will find the constituency evaluating alternatives." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 45% Republican Base & Its Economic Narrative", tag: "avoid", why: "The 45% Republican share in a Michigan suburban district reflects a meaningful constituency of homeowners, small business owners, and working-class voters evaluating on taxes, regulation, and perceived government overreach. At $75k median income, this group is not wealthy—they are middle-income households sensitive to property tax increases and skeptical of government spending efficiency. A Democratic incumbent who campaigns as though the Republican opposition is simply a matter of ideology rather than material economic concern about costs and government scope will fail to understand what voters are actually weighing. Engaging this 45% as people with legitimate economic concerns—rather than dismissing them—is strategically necessary to prevent Republican consolidation from converting close races into Republican wins." }
