          "Address renter and younger-adult economic concerns directly: at 30% renter rate, one-third of the district faces housing costs that consume a disproportionate share of the $78,195 median income. Property tax fairness, rental regulation, and the pathway from renting to homeownership are material quality-of-life issues for a cohort whose turnout and candidate preference are the swing factors in a 61%-39% result that is not demographically predetermined.",
          "Engage the 11.5% AIAN population with institutional respect and specific policy commitment: whether this population is concentrated geographically or dispersed, it represents a genuine constituent base whose economic mobility, healthcare access, and civic participation patterns shape district outcomes. Treating this population as part of the core constituency, rather than assuming it defaults to Democratic margins, is both a governance obligation and an electoral baseline requirement.",
          "Do not assume the 61% represents a stable homeowner-dominant coalition: the combination of 34% college attainment, 30% renter rate, and $78,195 income creates a district where younger, more mobile constituents evaluate on material economic outcomes—not partisan inheritance. Any incumbent who consolidates educated homeowners but loses renter turnout to low engagement or opposition mobilization will find the 61% margin compressed faster than the demographic shift alone would predict."
