          { name: "Institutional Trust & Racial Justice in Policing, Housing, and Employment", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 42.85% Black and 39.35% white population, the district contains two communities with fundamentally different historical relationships to institutional trust, housing discrimination, employment access, and police conduct. The 74% Democratic margin reflects alignment on these issues, but that alignment is active, not passive — it depends on explicit legislative attention to racial justice in housing policy, criminal justice reform, and employment discrimination protection. A representative who frames these issues as 'racial issues' rather than foundational economic and safety governance is missing the opportunity to consolidate the coalition around a unified agenda." },
          { name: "Professional Services & College-Educated Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "The 40.6% college-educated population is significantly above national average and likely includes healthcare professionals, educators, engineers, and public employees whose economic concerns and policy priorities may differ from the service and manufacturing workforce. This community's stake in public education funding, healthcare access, and institutional effectiveness is real, but can become secondary to broader economic messages. A representative who engages this constituency specifically on education, professional workforce development, and healthcare access — rather than absorbing them into generic working-class messaging — builds more durable coalition strength among professionals who have exit options and real choice about community investment." },
          { name: "Treating 74% as a Safe Supermajority That Doesn't Require Active Constituency Maintenance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74-26 result is a strong margin, but in a district with internal coalition tensions — working-class versus professional, renter versus homeowner, Black versus white — the supermajority is the product of active alignment, not inherited loyalty. Any successor who campaigns as though the 74% is a floor rather than an achievement will find that ceiling erodes when governance fails to deliver on the material economic and justice concerns that hold the coalition together. The 26% opposition in a district this Democratic likely reflects real constituency dissatisfaction with specific governance failures or a coalition segment that feels abandoned." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Majority's Distinct Economic Vulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "At 40.4% renters in a district with $67,699 median income, roughly four in ten households lack the housing equity that shields homeowners from wage stagnation and market volatility. Renters in this income range are disproportionately vulnerable to displacement through eviction, rent increases, and redevelopment pressure — and their policy priorities (tenant protection, rent stabilization, development of affordable housing) may diverge significantly from the homeowner-majority narrative. A representative who campaigns primarily to the homeowner constituency or who treats housing as a homeowner-centric issue will miss the distinct constituency that represents the true economic precarity in the district." }
