          { name: "Fractured Affluent-Suburban Coalition", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 50-50 result in a district with 71% white, $104,041 income, and 54.1% college education should produce a 55-60% Republican margin based on demographic alone — the 10+ point gap signals that college-educated affluent suburban professionals have decoupled from traditional partisan voting. This is not a district of uncertain demographics; it is a district of certain demographics with uncertain political allegiance. Understanding precisely what issue constellation has fractured this alignment — whether education policy, social issues, institutional concerns, or local governance — is the single most important analytical task any candidate can undertake." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Governance Expectations", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 54.1% college attainment, MI-SD-9 has one of the highest concentrations of college-educated adults in any competitive district — a population that typically evaluates candidates on competence, institutional respect, and whether government delivers on specific priorities rather than on partisan brand loyalty. These voters have disposable income ($104,041), financial security, and the education to evaluate complex policy questions; they are persuadable by credible argument about outcomes, not by partisan activation. The candidate who engages this constituency on the specific governance concerns that college-educated professionals care about — education quality, fiscal management, institutional stability — builds durability in what is currently a fluid electorate." },
          { name: "Younger Professional & Renter Population", tag: "careful", why: "The 26.3% renter rate — significantly higher than most suburban districts of comparable income — suggests a meaningful population of younger professionals and mobile workers whose economic and lifestyle concerns may differ substantially from the homeowner-majority base. At $104,041 median income, many of these renters are not constrained by poverty but by choice: they prioritize walkability, professional opportunity, and flexibility over property ownership. This population's concerns about housing affordability, community amenities, and economic mobility may be activating if addressed directly, but are easily ignored if a candidate defaults to homeowner-focused messaging." },
          { name: "Partisan Base Mobilization as Primary Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50-50 result indicates that neither party has successfully mobilized its traditional base in this district — treating this election as though partisan activation will break the tie is arithmetically false. The candidate who treats MI-SD-9 as though the outcome depends on turning out more Republicans or more Democrats is misdiagnosing a district where the decisive margin lies among voters who are genuinely cross-pressured and persuadable. Partisan messaging will not move a constituency that has already shown it does not respond to inherited partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Assuming Current 50-50 Equilibrium Is Stable", tag: "avoid", why: "A tied result is not a stable equilibrium; it is an unstable one where the next election could move significantly in either direction depending on which candidate better addresses the specific concerns that have fractured the traditional coalition. The candidate who campaigns as if 50% is a floor and the remaining voters are naturally persuadable to their side is making a strategic error; the 50-50 result indicates that persuasion is equally possible for either direction. This is a genuinely competitive district where the outcome depends on who better diagnoses and addresses what has broken the traditional alignment." }
