        memoHeadline: "Michigan Senate District 10: A Racially Balanced, Working-Class District at 68% Democratic — Sustained by Black and White Coalition, Not Demographic Destiny",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "MI-SD-10 presents a rare demographic symmetry in American politics: 45.11% white and 44.69% Black residents, with median household income of $56,356, a 36.7-year median age, and 19.7% college attainment. This is a genuinely integrated working-class community where neither demographic group constitutes a safe electoral majority — the district's 68% Democratic result is not a function of one group's size, but rather of sustained coalition-building across both communities around shared economic interests. The 35.5% renter rate signals substantial housing instability and cost-of-living pressure; at $56,356 median income with over one-third of households renting, this is a constituency for whom housing affordability, wage stagnation, and the cost of childcare and healthcare are not abstract policy concerns but immediate financial threats. Wojno's 68% margin reflects successful alignment with both the Black community's civic infrastructure and white working-class voters' economic security concerns — a coalition that is durable only if maintained through specific attention to shared material interests rather than assumed loyalty from either demographic group.",
          "The electoral vulnerability in this district lies in the assumption that a 68% margin is stable or self-sustaining. At 45-45 racial balance with below-median college attainment and renter-heavy housing patterns, MI-SD-10 is composed of voters who evaluate performance on whether government is delivering tangible improvements to their economic position — not on inherited partisan affiliation or demographic identity alone. The Black community's approximately 45% share and the white community's approximately 45% share mean that either demographic could shift materially if a credible alternative message reaches them. White working-class voters at $56,356 income are responsive to candidates who address wage and job quality without racial resentment framing; Black voters in this district are responsive to candidates who demonstrate material commitment to housing stability, healthcare access, and criminal justice reform. Any challenger who can credibly appeal to economic frustration among either group while maintaining the other's support threatens the current 68% coalition. Conversely, any incumbent who takes either community's support for granted — by neglecting to deliver on specific material commitments or by allowing messaging to drift away from economic substance — creates opening for a primary or general election challenge."
        ],
