          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost for Affluent, Educated Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $92,991 median income and 39.7% college education, MI-SD-23's core constituency is affluent and educated enough to experience healthcare costs as a direct material concern rather than an abstract policy issue. Premiums, deductibles, prescription drug costs, and quality of care are governance issues that educated professionals evaluate against incumbent performance and challenger positions with specificity. A representative who addresses healthcare cost containment, insurance market regulation, and quality-of-care outcomes with concrete policy fluency is building the credibility this educated constituency demands; one who defaults to ideology alone is vulnerable to a challenger who takes these material concerns seriously." },
          { name: "Education Funding and Quality in Affluent Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.7% college-educated population in MI-SD-23 is substantially higher than state average and reflects a community where education attainment is both a source of economic status and a measure of community quality. School funding adequacy, teacher retention, curriculum quality, and the local school system's competitiveness with peer districts are not abstract policy concerns for this constituency—they are direct reflections on property values, their children's preparation, and the community's desirability. A representative who champions education funding and can speak to specific improvements in local schools has concrete credibility; one who campaigns on education cuts or ideological battles over curriculum faces an engaged, educated opposition." },
          { name: "College-Educated Voter Persuadability and Demographic Drift", tag: "careful", why: "The 39.7% college-educated population in a 59-41 Republican district is notably higher than the national college-educated Republican average, suggesting that this constituency is not uniformly partisan but rather persuadable on specific issues where educated voters' material interests diverge from party positioning. Environmental regulation, healthcare cost containment, and social policy issues where educated voters trend more liberal than working-class Republicans are predictable swing factors in a district with these demographics. The 59-41 result holds only if college-educated voters perceive the incumbent as responsive to their specific concerns; any drift in this segment toward Democratic opposition is mathematically significant in a district where college-educated voters are nearly 40% of the population." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Divergent from Local Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 district with $92,991 income and 39.7% college education is affluent and educated enough that ideologically driven positions disconnected from local material governance concerns lose credibility with the core constituency. Positioning that appeals to working-class Republican social issues at the expense of addressing healthcare costs, education funding, or environmental protection where affluent, educated professionals have direct economic stakes is a vulnerability that a well-resourced Democratic challenger can exploit explicitly and effectively." },
          { name: "Dismissing Democratic Opposition as Unserious or Non-Credible", tag: "avoid", why: "A 41% Democratic result in a district with affluent, educated professionals reflects genuine opposition from constituents with the resources and civic engagement to pose a credible electoral challenge. Treating this opposition as marginal, dismissing Democratic candidates' positions without substantive engagement, or campaigning as though the 59% is a secure floor creates opening for a challenger to position as genuinely responsive to constituent concerns the incumbent dismisses. In an educated constituency, this strategy is counterproductive; engagement with the opposition's specific arguments is necessary to maintain the 59-41 margin." }
