          { name: "Homeowner Property Values & Neighborhood Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 87.4% homeownership and a 44.2-year median age, this is a district where homeowners are the overwhelming majority and property-holder concerns are genuinely central to constituent economic security. Property tax fairness, school funding that protects residential property values, and neighborhood infrastructure investment are the material governance concerns for a constituency whose largest asset is almost certainly their home. Messaging that centers on protecting and enhancing property values, neighborhood safety, and quality of life for homeowners consolidates the existing Republican base and signals respect for the constituency's primary economic stake." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Swing Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 39.6% college attainment rate in a district at 66% Republican is the demographic signal of an educated constituency that is not fully consolidated; college-educated voters nationwide have demonstrated capacity to swing on education funding, healthcare policy, and environmental concerns. At 34% Democratic support in a district this white and affluent, the opposition is likely concentrated among college-educated professionals evaluating on these specific issues rather than inherited partisan loyalty. Any challenger who can mobilize this constituency around healthcare or education messaging poses a genuine threat, and any incumbent who neglects to demonstrate responsiveness to college-educated voter concerns risks ceding this swing group." },
          { name: "Framing the District as Economically Secure Without Qualification", tag: "avoid", why: "While MI-SD-24's $104,936 median income is genuinely high, aggregate income conceals individual and household-level anxiety about cost-of-living inflation, healthcare sustainability, and intergenerational economic opportunity. Any messaging that treats the district's wealth as equivalent to immunity from economic concerns or cost-of-living pressure will ring false to professionals actually paying healthcare bills, property taxes, and college tuition. A 66% margin held by consolidating the affluent homeowner majority is only sustainable if those homeowners feel their cost-of-living concerns are acknowledged and addressed." },
          { name: "Taking the 66% Margin as Permission to Neglect the Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 split in a district this demographically Republican — 88.46% white, 87.4% homeowner, $104,936 income, 39.6% college-educated — is not overwhelming; it is a margin that suggests 34% of the district's most likely Republican voters are actively choosing an alternative. That opposition, if concentrated among college-educated professionals and mobilized around healthcare, education, or environmental concerns, represents a real swing constituency that could close the margin to 55-45 or flip the seat entirely in a wave cycle. Treating 66% as a floor rather than as a stable majority is the strategic error most likely to produce a surprise loss." }
