          { name: "Economic Opportunity for Younger & Renter Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.1% renter rate and 47% Democratic opposition in a 79% homeowner district suggests that younger adults and renters face distinct economic barriers — housing affordability, wage stagnation, or limited local employment pathways — that the incumbent may not be addressing. Young adults who cannot transition from renting to homeownership, or who perceive limited economic future in the district, are a persuadable swing constituency if offered specific policy attention to wages, housing costs, and local economic development that retains younger talent." },
          { name: "American Indian/Alaska Native Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 5.6% demographic gap in MI-SD-32 represents an American Indian/Alaska Native population that is statistically present but strategically invisible in current analysis. In Michigan districts with tribal populations, voter mobilization around specific issues — healthcare access, tribal sovereignty, economic development — has proven capable of shifting close races. At 53-47, a modest increase in tribal turnout or a shift in candidate perception could alter the outcome; this population requires explicit constituent relationship-building rather than assumption of behavior." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result is a narrow plurality, not a secure majority, in a district where 47% of voters actively chose someone else. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns MI-SD-32 as though 53% represents a comfortable hold is arithmetically misreading the strategic reality; this is a competitive seat that demands active constituent engagement and specific policy remedies for the opposition's driving concerns, not the quiet confidence of a landslide strategy." },
          { name: "Dismissing Low College Education as Simple Partisanship", tag: "avoid", why: "The 23.1% college-education rate should not be read as a reliable partisan indicator — it signals a working-class and trade-skilled constituency that evaluates heavily on material economic outcomes and concrete local governance performance. A legislator who assumes this population's partisan loyalty rather than earning it with specific attention to job quality, cost-of-living management, and local economic development misses the strategic vulnerability that the 47% represents." }
