          { name: "Homeowner Financial Security & Cost-of-Living Pressures", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $72,537 median household income with 84.2% homeownership, MI-SD-33 is a district of working-class and middle-class homeowners for whom property tax burdens, energy costs, and commuting expenses represent real monthly financial pressure. In exurban Michigan's cost environment, this income level does not provide wide discretionary margin, and households are vulnerable to inflation, utility rate increases, and service-delivery costs that hit rural areas harder than urban corridors. A representative who can deliver concrete property tax fairness, energy cost management, and rural infrastructure investment speaks directly to the material concerns that determine whether this 66% holds or erodes." },
          { name: "Rural Service Delivery & Infrastructure Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 21.7% college-educated population and $72,537 income signal a working-class exurban constituency dependent on vocational skills, manufacturing, small business, and agricultural activities — not credentialed professional employment. Rural service delivery — water and sewer infrastructure, road maintenance, broadband access, emergency response — are the practical governance requirements for a community at this income and education level. Outman holds 66% only if rural constituents perceive that state investment is flowing to their community rather than being concentrated in urban centers; neglecting this dimension in favor of abstract conservative messaging leaves the seat exposed." },
          { name: "The ~4.5% Demographic Gap & Unaccounted Population", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories total 95.44%, leaving approximately 4.5% of the district population unaccounted for — roughly 3,000-4,000 residents depending on district population. In Michigan exurban context, this gap most likely represents American Indian/Alaska Native communities, recent immigrant populations, or mixed-race households whose civic engagement and turnout patterns are not yet fully mapped in the existing partisan result. In a 66-34 district, this population's turnout and candidate preference in a high-engagement cycle could shift the margin by 2-3 percentage points; any candidate should invest in understanding who this population is and building genuine constituent relationship rather than assuming they remain disengaged." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Durable Partisan Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result in a district where 34% actively chose an alternative — and where underlying economic conditions are creating real household financial pressure — should not be interpreted as a safe seat or a durable Republican hold. In high-turnout cycles, or if a credible challenger emerges who speaks directly to homeowner cost-of-living concerns while engaging the unaccounted-for population, this margin can compress quickly. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 66% represents security rather than a current plurality in a contested district will be vulnerable to a well-executed challenger campaign." },
          { name: "Messaging That Dismisses Cost-of-Living Concerns as 'Inflation' or 'Federal Policy'", tag: "avoid", why: "While inflation is a real macroeconomic phenomenon, households experiencing rising property taxes, energy bills, and service costs experience it as a local governance failure — a failure to manage costs, invest efficiently, or protect homeowner financial security. Any representative who responds to constituent cost-of-living pressure with abstract blaming of federal policy or macroeconomic forces will be perceived as dismissive of material household concerns. This district's 34% opposition likely includes voters who evaluated on these grounds and found the current representative's response inadequate; repeating that messaging will not consolidate the 66%." }
